scope:In scope: Apple Developer portal mutations for exactly the four approved macOS identifiers; the Network Extensions capability on all four, hosts included; development certificates selected by authorized operators; this Apple-silicon Mac inclusion; Mac Development profile generation and download; inspection of profile UUID, team, application identifier, entitlements, devices and expiry; asserting the unsuffixed packet-tunnel-provider value on host and provider profiles alike; asserting the matrix negatives, namely that no App Groups capability is enabled on any of the four App IDs and that no generated profile Entitlements dict carries com.apple.security.application-groups (assertion A16). Out of scope: creating any App Group record; enabling the App Groups capability anywhere; any Keychain access-group portal mutation; provisioning the four deferred iOS identifiers; any Developer ID, App Store or other distribution profile; any change to the shipped legacy macOS bundle identity; committing profiles or certificates; TestFlight; notarization; unrelated capabilities; product code.
ac:1. Exactly four macOS App IDs exist under the expected Relux Works team - works.relux.tunnel.mac, works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel, works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac and works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac.tunnel - and match the approved naming matrix exactly; no App Group record is created; the four iOS identifiers stay defined in the matrix and are deliberately not provisioned because iOS is deferred under ADR-024. 2. The Network Extensions capability is enabled on all four App IDs, hosts included, and every generated Mac Development profile authorizes the unsuffixed packet-tunnel-provider value on host and provider profiles alike; the App Groups capability is enabled on none of the four; no Keychain access-group mutation is performed, and the team-wide keychain-access-groups wildcard that every Mac Development profile carries is expected and is not a finding. 3. One Mac Development profile per App ID is generated for this Apple-silicon Mac context, with the iPhone profile recorded as a named deferred gap under ADR-024, and each inspects to the expected team, application identifier, entitlements, devices and validity. 4. Reproduction steps and privacy-safe profile metadata are attached as a TASK-ID-scoped outcome resource; raw profiles, certificates and private keys are not attached or committed. 5. Any portal limitation or capability requiring Apple approval is recorded as an explicit external blocker rather than bypassed.
